        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "In parameter-column order:\n$x$: $-4,-3,-2,-1,0$.\n$y$: $-5,-3,-1,1,3$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $t=-2,-1,0,1,2$ into each equation.\n$x=t-2$: $-4,-3,-2,-1,0$.\n$y=2t-1$: $-5,-3,-1,1,3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0,
        "responseSpace": "scaffold"
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q4b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain, using the table, why the graph is a straight line.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Every increase of $1$ in $x$ gives an increase of $2$ in $y$, so the gradient is constant.",
          "worked": "As $x$ changes, $y$ changes by a constant amount. In this case, as $x$ increases by $1$, $y$ increases by $2$. Therefore, the graph is a straight line.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q4c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "From the table, find the $y$-intercept and gradient.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y$-intercept: $3$; gradient: $2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}m&=\\frac{-3-(-5)}{-3-(-4)}\\\\m&=2\\end{aligned}$$\nIn the last column, $x=0$ and $y=3$, so the $y$-intercept is $3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q4d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Eliminate $t$ to find the Cartesian equation and check it with part c.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=2x+3$; gradient $2$ and $y$-intercept $3$, agreeing with part c.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}t&=x+2\\end{aligned}$$\nSubstitute into $y=2t-1$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y&=2(x+2)-1\\\\y&=2x+3\\end{aligned}$$\nThe gradient is $2$ and the $y$-intercept is $3$, agreeing with part c.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      }
